<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rathwa people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rathwa are an Adivasi community of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, living mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhota-udaipur">Chhota Udaipur</a> district and neighbouring parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Mostly farmers in the forested hills, they are known for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pithora-painting">Pithora paintings</a> made on the walls of their houses to honour the deity Baba Pithora, and for their spring festivals around Holi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rathwa.jpg" alt="Rathwa people" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Suyash Dwivedi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rathva_Men_Women_Community_Gujrat_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
